# y5: extra sections + FAQs for the y1-y4 guides
def _ext(slug, extra, faq=()):
  t, ps = LONG[slug]; ps=list(ps); LONG[slug]=(t, ps[:-1]+extra+ps[-1:]); FAQX[slug]=list(FAQX.get(slug,[]))+list(faq)
_ext('free-12x36-album-psd-download',[
"## Building a full album from free sheets",
"A complete wedding album usually needs 20 to 40 spreads, and no single free pack covers every event. Plan the album in sections first: engagement, getting ready, ceremony, family portraits, reception and closing. Then choose one main design family for the ceremony and portraits, and a lighter companion style for haldi, mehendi or reception pages. Matching the font pair and margin width across both families keeps the book feeling consistent even when the backgrounds change.",
"### A simple sheet plan",
"- **Cover and opening spread:** strongest couple portrait, names and date.",
"- **Event spreads:** one hero image plus three to six supporting photos each.",
"- **Family collages:** tighter grids so every relative appears without dozens of pages.",
"- **Plain background sheets:** rest pages for single large portraits.",
"Keep a spreadsheet or notebook listing each sheet number, its template and the photos used. It makes client revisions much easier and stops the same photo appearing twice.",
"## Common mistakes with free templates",
"The biggest mistakes are using a template at the wrong size and leaving demo photos hidden but not deleted, which bloats the file and sometimes prints by accident if a layer is switched on. Delete placeholder photos once yours are in. Also check that no template text such as sample names or lorem ipsum remains on any sheet before export.",
],[("How many sheets are in a typical 12x36 wedding album?","Most range from 20 to 40 spreads depending on the number of events and the client's budget."),("Should I mix different template styles in one album?","You can, but keep the same fonts and margins so the album still feels like one book.")])
_ext('how-to-edit-karizma-album-psd',[
"## Troubleshooting common Karizma editing problems",
"### Photo looks blurry inside the frame",
"The source photo is too small or was scaled up as a normal layer. Replace it with the original camera file placed as a Smart Object, and check at 100 percent zoom. A photo filling a 12 inch tall frame needs at least 3600 pixels in height.",
"### Clipping mask clips to the wrong layer",
"A clipping mask always clips to the layer directly below. If your photo disappears or shows in the wrong shape, drag it so it sits immediately above the correct frame layer in the Layers panel.",
"### Colours change after saving",
"This usually happens when the document profile differs from the export profile. Check Edit > Assign Profile; most Karizma templates are sRGB or Adobe RGB. Convert to sRGB before exporting JPGs.",
"### Ornaments look jagged in print",
"Some templates use low-resolution decorative PNGs. Replace them with sharper versions from another sheet in the same family, or scale them down slightly so their effective resolution rises.",
"## A repeatable editing routine",
"Once you have edited a few Karizma sheets, settle into the same routine every time: lock decoration, place photos, clip, colour match, edit text, check margins, save, export. Following the same order reduces mistakes when you are designing thirty sheets in a weekend during wedding season.",
],[("Why is my Karizma PSD file so large?","Karizma sheets are high resolution with many texture and photo layers. Resize photos before placing to keep the file manageable."),("Can I change the background colour of a Karizma template?","Yes. Clip a Hue/Saturation or Color Balance adjustment layer to the background group instead of painting over it.")])
_ext('12x18-album-size-guide',[
"## Designing layouts for a 12x18 sheet",
"A 12x18 sheet has about half the area of a 12x36 spread, so layouts need to be simpler. Two to five photos per sheet usually works best. A common structure is one large photo covering about two thirds of the sheet and two or three smaller photos stacked beside it. For portrait-orientation sheets, a single full-height portrait with a narrow strip of details at the bottom is elegant.",
"### Text and margins",
"Margins of 0.25 to 0.4 inch are enough on a 12x18 sheet. Names and captions can be smaller than on a 12x36 spread, but keep body text at 14 points or above so it remains readable in print.",
"## Common 12x18 mistakes",
"- **Designing at 12x36 and splitting later:** layouts rarely divide cleanly, and the fold area causes awkward gaps.",
"- **Too many photos:** small frames make faces tiny on a 12x18 sheet.",
"- **Wrong orientation:** confirm landscape or portrait before you start, especially if the lab has a fixed binding direction.",
"- **Low DPI exports:** some designers export at 72 DPI by mistake; always check Image Size before saving.",
"## Pricing tip for studios",
"Because 12x18 books use less material, they make good add-ons: a parents' copy, a pre-wedding book or a birthday album. See our [parents' album design guide](/blog/parents-album-design-guide/) for ideas on reusing main-album work.",
],[("How many photos fit on a 12x18 sheet?","Two to five photos per sheet usually looks best; more makes faces too small."),("What margin should I use on a 12x18 album sheet?","Keep text and faces at least 0.25 to 0.4 inch from the edges to allow for trimming.")])
_ext('18x24-album-size-guide',[
"## Layout ideas for 18x24 sheets",
"### Single hero portrait",
"A full-bleed portrait covering the whole sheet with names placed in an empty area of the photo makes a striking wall print or album opener. Choose a photo with natural negative space, such as sky, a plain wall or a soft background.",
"### Grid of six",
"For event albums, a 3x2 grid of equal frames with even gutters of about 0.25 inch gives a clean, editorial look that suits large group photos.",
"### Panel with caption",
"Place one large image on about 70 percent of the sheet and use the remaining strip for a short caption, date and two small detail photos.",
"## Preparing photos for large prints",
"Large sheets reveal noise and soft focus. Use noise reduction carefully in Camera Raw before placing photos, and apply print sharpening at the end rather than at the start. A photo for a full 18x24 print at 300 DPI needs about 7200 pixels on its long side; most modern cameras deliver 6000 to 8000 pixels, so crop sparingly.",
"## Working with mixed-size albums",
"Some studios combine 18x24 hero sheets with smaller inner pages in a box set rather than a bound book. If you do this, keep fonts and colours identical across sizes so the set feels unified, and number sheets clearly for the lab.",
"## File handling",
"18x24 documents with many layers can exceed 2 GB. If Photoshop refuses to save as PSD, use File > Save As and choose Large Document Format (PSB). Our guide on [fixing slow Photoshop with large album PSDs](/blog/photoshop-slow-large-album-psd/) covers performance settings.",
],[("Can I print an 18x24 album sheet from a phone photo?","Only from recent high-resolution phone cameras in good light, and even then check sharpness at 100 percent before printing."),("Should 18x24 sheets be portrait or landscape?","Either works. Portrait suits single portraits; landscape suits groups and venue shots.")])
_ext('colour-grading-wedding-album-photos',[
"## A sample grading workflow",
"Here is a simple order many album designers follow for a full wedding:",
"- **Cull and organise:** remove duplicates and blurry frames first.",
"- **Base correction:** white balance, exposure and lens correction on all photos.",
"- **Match cameras:** if two photographers shot the wedding, adjust one camera's colours to match the other using a reference photo of the same scene.",
"- **Apply the grade:** sync your album preset across all photos.",
"- **Event tweaks:** adjust strength for night, indoor and outdoor scenes.",
"- **Skin check:** review portraits at 100 percent.",
"- **Export:** sRGB JPGs sized for album placement.",
"## Mistakes that ruin grading",
"### Over-saturated reds",
"Bridal sarees and kumkum are already intense reds. Pushing saturation makes them clip and lose texture in print. Reduce red saturation slightly instead.",
"### Heavy teal and orange",
"The popular cinematic look can turn skin orange and green decorations murky. Use it gently, if at all, for traditional ceremonies.",
"### Different grades per photographer",
"When multiple shooters deliver photos, mismatched grades are obvious on a spread where their images sit side by side. Always grade the whole set together.",
"## Saving and reusing your look",
"Save successful grades as named presets, for example Warm Temple or Clean Daylight, and note which album templates they suit. Over time you will build a small, reliable set that speeds up every new album.",
],[("Should I use the same preset for every wedding?","Use a consistent starting preset for your style, but adjust strength and white balance for each wedding's lighting."),("Can I grade photos after placing them in the album?","Yes, with clipped adjustment layers in Photoshop, but grading in Lightroom or Camera Raw before placing is faster for large sets.")])
_ext('smart-objects-album-design',[
"## A practical example: reusing a spread",
"Imagine you have designed a spread with one large couple portrait and four smaller photos. Every photo is a Smart Object clipped into a frame. For the next wedding:",
"- **Duplicate the PSD** and rename it for the new client.",
"- **Right-click each photo** and choose Replace Contents, selecting the new client's image.",
"- **Adjust framing** with Free Transform if faces sit differently.",
"- **Update text** for names and date.",
"- **Recolour** the background with a clipped Hue/Saturation layer if needed.",
"The whole spread can be ready in a few minutes, and any Smart Filters such as a gentle Camera Raw adjustment carry across automatically.",
"## Editing Smart Object contents safely",
"Double-clicking a Smart Object opens its contents as a temporary .psb file. Make your changes, then save and close that file; the main document updates. Do not use Save As inside the temporary file, as the main document will not update. If you want to break the link between duplicated Smart Objects, use Layer > Smart Objects > New Smart Object via Copy, so editing one no longer changes the others.",
"## Smart Objects and vector elements",
"Logos, ornaments and text effects created in Illustrator can be placed as vector Smart Objects. They stay crisp at any size, which is ideal for monograms on covers and title pages. Avoid third-party brand logos in client albums unless you have permission.",
],[("Why does my edited Smart Object not update?","Make sure you save the temporary .psb file with Save, not Save As, before closing it."),("Should I use linked or embedded Smart Objects?","Embedded is safer for archiving and sharing; linked keeps files smaller when everything stays on one computer.")])
_ext('photoshop-actions-for-album-designers',[
"## Example: a complete export action",
"Here is a step list for an action that exports a print copy without touching your master PSD:",
"- **Duplicate the document** (Image > Duplicate, merged layers only).",
"- **Convert to profile** sRGB IEC61966-2.1.",
"- **Convert to 8 bits per channel** if the file is 16-bit.",
"- **Save As JPG** at quality 12 into a Print_JPG folder.",
"- **Close the duplicate** without saving changes.",
"Because the action works on a duplicate, the layered original stays open and unchanged. Assign it to a function key such as F12 and export each finished sheet with one press.",
"## Organising action sets",
"Create separate sets for setup, photo prep, retouching and export. Name actions clearly with the size or purpose, for example 12x36 New Sheet or Export sRGB Q12. Delete old experiments regularly so the panel stays easy to scan, and switch to Button Mode from the panel menu if you prefer clickable buttons.",
"## Troubleshooting actions",
"### The command is not currently available",
"An action step expects a specific layer or selection that does not exist in the current document. Add a Stop before that step or record a layer selection at the start.",
"### Wrong folder on another computer",
"Save steps record full paths. On a new computer, re-record the save step or use the batch dialog's destination folder override.",
],[("How do I assign a shortcut key to an action?","Double-click the action name area or choose Action Options from the panel menu and select a function key."),("Can actions open files from a folder automatically?","Yes, using File > Automate > Batch, which runs an action on every file in a chosen folder.")])
_ext('export-album-sheets-jpg-for-print',[
"## Export As vs Save a Copy",
"Photoshop offers two main routes. Export As is fast and supports batch export of artboards, but by default it can omit resolution metadata and may convert colours to sRGB without telling you. Save a Copy (or Save As in older versions) with JPG keeps the resolution value, embeds the profile you choose and lets you set quality 0 to 12. For print work, Save a Copy is the more predictable option.",
"### What about PNG and TIFF?",
"PNG is lossless but rarely accepted by album labs and creates huge files at 10800 x 3600 pixels. TIFF is accepted by some premium labs; use LZW compression and flatten layers. If in doubt, ask the lab for its preferred format.",
"## File size expectations",
"A 12x36 JPG at quality 12 usually weighs 15 to 40 MB, depending on detail. If your files are only 2 to 3 MB, check that you have not exported at low quality or reduced size by mistake. If they are over 60 MB, check for unnecessary noise or very detailed textures.",
"## Archiving after delivery",
"Once the lab confirms the files, archive the layered PSDs, final JPGs, fonts used and the client's approved proof together. Store at least two copies on different drives. Clients often return months later for a parents' copy or a reprint, and an organised archive turns that into quick, easy work.",
"## A final quality pass",
"Before zipping, view all JPGs as a slideshow at full screen. Seeing them in sequence reveals repeated photos, uneven brightness between sheets and inconsistent text positions more clearly than checking files one by one.",
],[("Why is my exported JPG showing 72 DPI?","Export As can drop resolution metadata. Use Save a Copy as JPG or set the resolution in Image Size without resampling."),("How big should a 12x36 album JPG file be?","Usually between 15 and 40 MB at maximum quality, depending on image detail.")])
_ext('skin-retouching-for-album-photos',[
"## A five-minute portrait routine",
"For album volume, speed matters. A practical routine for a close-up portrait:",
"- **One minute:** global Camera Raw adjustments and slight negative Texture.",
"- **Two minutes:** spot healing of blemishes and stray hairs on a separate layer.",
"- **One minute:** quick dodge and burn on the most obvious shadows and shine.",
"- **One minute:** eyes, teeth and a final check at 100 percent.",
"Save the layer setup as a Photoshop action so the empty healing layer and the two Curves layers appear automatically.",
"## Retouching group photos",
"Groups need lighter treatment. Remove obvious distractions such as a passing guest, a bright exit sign or a fan in the background, and correct closed eyes by swapping heads from another frame in the burst if available. Use Auto-Align Layers to line up the frames, then mask in the better face.",
"## Respecting client wishes",
"Always ask whether the couple has any preferences. Some want minimal retouching; others ask for specific changes. Avoid altering skin tone or body shape unless explicitly requested, and keep the originals so you can revert if the family prefers a more natural look.",
"## Checking retouching in print",
"Retouching that looks perfect on screen can look patchy in print, especially on matte paper. Include one closely retouched portrait in your test print to confirm the texture looks natural at the final size.",
],[("Can I use AI tools for skin retouching?","AI tools can speed up blemish removal, but check results closely; they can over-smooth skin or alter features. Read our guide on using AI in album design."),("How do I fix closed eyes in a group photo?","Use another frame from the same burst, align the layers and mask in the open-eyed face.")])
_ext('fix-missing-fonts-in-psd',[
"## Choosing reliable replacement fonts",
"When you cannot find the original, pick a replacement with a similar structure rather than a similar name. Look at the x-height, letter width and stroke contrast. For elegant scripts, choose one with connected letters and moderate swashes; very decorative scripts are hard to read for long names. For serif titles, classic high-contrast serifs replace each other well. Google Fonts and Adobe Fonts offer many families with licences that cover commercial use.",
"## When text is already rasterised",
"Sometimes template text is flattened into pixels and cannot be edited. In that case, hide or delete the pixel layer, recreate the text with the Type tool, and match size and colour using the Eyedropper. Copy layer styles from another text layer if the original had a gold gradient or shadow.",
"## Checking before export",
"Missing fonts can cause problems at the last minute. Before exporting print JPGs, open Type > Manage Missing Fonts once more. If it lists nothing, every text layer is rendering with an installed font. Then zoom through each text layer at 100 percent to check spacing, because replaced fonts often shift kerning slightly.",
"## Sharing PSDs with other designers",
"If you hand off PSDs to another designer or a lab that edits files, include the fonts or a list with download links where the licence allows, or convert final text to shapes on a copy. This avoids the same missing-font problem on their computer.",
],[("Where can I find fonts that are safe for commercial album work?","Adobe Fonts and Google Fonts offer many families licensed for commercial use. Always check each font's licence."),("Can I edit text that has been rasterised in a template?","Not directly. Hide the pixel layer and recreate the text with the Type tool, matching size and colour.")])
_ext('puberty-ceremony-album-design',[
"## Working with ceremony lighting",
"Many puberty functions take place at home or in small halls with mixed lighting: tube lights, LED stage lights and daylight from windows. Photos from the same ritual may have very different colours. Correct white balance event by event before design, and use a consistent warm grade to tie the album together. Our [colour correction tips](/blog/album-colour-correction-tips/) and [colour grading guide](/blog/colour-grading-wedding-album-photos/) cover the process.",
"## Respectful photo selection",
"Choose photos where she looks comfortable and confident. Avoid unflattering candid moments during rituals, and ask the family before including any photo that shows her emotional or tired. A short review of the shortlist with the mother or the girl herself prevents awkward reprints later.",
"## Gift copies for relatives",
"Grandparents and maternal uncles often appreciate a small album of their own. A 12x18 book with 10 to 15 sheets, focused on family portraits and blessing moments, is a thoughtful add-on that studios can offer at a modest price. See our [parents' album design guide](/blog/parents-album-design-guide/) for reusing layouts efficiently.",
"## Cover design",
"For the cover, choose a single elegant portrait and keep text minimal. A soft floral or golden border frames it well. Browse the [album cover category](/category/album-cover/) for front panel designs and read our [album cover design guide](/blog/album-cover-design-guide/).",
],[("What colours suit a puberty ceremony album?","Turmeric yellow, maroon, green and gold suit traditional functions; pastel pink and peach suit modern families."),("Should the album include ritual photos?","Include the main rituals the family values, but choose photos where she looks comfortable and check sensitive images with the family.")])
_ext('anniversary-album-design-ideas',[
"## Collecting photos from the family",
"For surprise albums, the hardest part is gathering photos. Ask children to collect prints from relatives and send them in one batch, with years and places written on the back or listed in a message. Phone photos of prints are rarely sharp enough; a flatbed scan or a careful photograph in daylight with the camera parallel to the print gives far better results.",
"### Restoring damaged prints",
"Creases, tears and stains can be repaired with the Spot Healing Brush and Clone Stamp at low opacity on a separate layer. For heavily damaged photos, focus repairs on faces and leave minor background damage; it keeps the photo authentic and saves time.",
"## Designing the timeline spread",
"A timeline spread works well across a wide 12x36 sheet. Draw a thin line along the lower third, place small photos above it at even intervals, and add the year below each one in a clean font. Five to eight milestones fit comfortably. Keep the line and text in a muted colour so the photos remain the focus.",
"## The anniversary function itself",
"If there is a function, treat it like a small wedding chapter: venue details, the couple arriving, cake or pooja, garland exchange or vow renewal, family portraits and guests. Two to five spreads are usually enough to complete the story without overshadowing the archive photos.",
],[("How many old photos should an anniversary album include?","It depends on the story, but 30 to 60 well-chosen archive photos alongside recent ones usually works well."),("Can I enlarge an old 4x6 print for an album page?","Yes, if it is scanned at 600 DPI or higher. Larger enlargements may look soft, so place old photos in smaller frames.")])
_ext('haldi-mehendi-album-pages',[
"## Sangeet and cocktail nights",
"Many weddings combine mehendi with a sangeet or follow it with a cocktail night. These evenings bring stage lights, dance performances and dark backgrounds. Use darker templates with subtle gold or bokeh textures, and choose photos with clear faces; dramatic stage lighting can leave faces in shadow. A full-width dance photo across a 12x36 spread captures the energy well, provided no face sits on the fold.",
"## Detail shots that tell the story",
"Haldi and mehendi pages come alive with details: the turmeric bowl, marigold strings, bangles, the mehendi cone, sweets and decorated swings. Group four to six details into a strip or grid on one side of a spread to balance a large candid photo on the other side.",
"## Consistency with the main album",
"Even though these pages are more playful, keep the same font family, margin width and gutter spacing as the ceremony chapters. Viewers should sense a change in mood without feeling they have opened a different book. A small chapter title using the album's script font is enough to mark the transition.",
"## Common mistakes",
"- **Too many similar candids:** choose the best expression from each burst.",
"- **Yellow overload:** avoid yellow backgrounds behind haldi photos.",
"- **Missing the mehendi design:** always include one sharp close-up of the full pattern.",
],[("Which templates suit mehendi pages?","Floral and jewel-toned templates suit mehendi; light cream templates suit haldi."),("Can I combine haldi and mehendi on one spread?","For smaller albums, yes. Use a clear visual divide and a short title for each event.")])
_ext('monitor-calibration-for-album-designers',[
"## Colour settings in Photoshop",
"Calibration works best when Photoshop's colour settings are consistent. Go to Edit > Color Settings and choose a preset such as North America General Purpose or Europe General Purpose, which uses sRGB as the RGB working space. Tick the options to ask when opening files with mismatched profiles, so you know when a template uses Adobe RGB. Convert those files to sRGB before export if your lab prints sRGB.",
"## Soft proofing",
"If your lab provides an ICC profile for its printer and paper, install it and use View > Proof Setup > Custom to simulate the print on screen. Soft proofing helps you spot colours that will shift, such as intense saree reds or saturated stage lights. Many Indian labs do not share profiles, in which case a test print remains the most reliable check.",
"## Laptops and second screens",
"Laptop screens vary widely and many cover only part of sRGB. If you edit on a laptop, connect a calibrated external monitor for colour decisions and use the laptop screen only for panels and tools. Avoid judging colour on phones; their brightness and colour settings change automatically.",
"## Signs your monitor needs attention",
"- **Prints always darker:** screen brightness too high.",
"- **Prints warmer or yellower:** screen white point too cool or blue.",
"- **Shadows blocked up in print:** gamma or black level set incorrectly.",
"- **Different results on two monitors:** at least one is not calibrated.",
],[("What brightness should a monitor be for print editing?","Around 100 to 120 cd/m², much lower than most monitors' default brightness."),("Do I need a wide-gamut monitor for album design?","No. A good sRGB IPS monitor is enough for most album work, as labs typically print from sRGB files.")])
_ext('photoshop-slow-large-album-psd',[
"## A quick diagnostic checklist",
"When Photoshop slows down, work through this list before changing hardware:",
"- **Check Efficiency:** in the status bar at the bottom of the document, choose Efficiency. Below 100 percent means Photoshop is using the scratch disk heavily.",
"- **Check document size:** the status bar also shows document size. If it is several gigabytes, resize photos or remove hidden layers.",
"- **Close other documents:** every open PSD uses memory.",
"- **Close background apps:** browsers with many tabs and video editors compete for RAM.",
"- **Restart Photoshop:** long sessions accumulate memory use; a restart often restores speed.",
"## Reset preferences if things break",
"If Photoshop becomes unstable even with small files, preferences may be corrupted. Hold Alt+Ctrl+Shift (Option+Cmd+Shift on Mac) immediately after launching Photoshop and confirm deleting the settings file. Note your custom shortcuts and workspaces first, because they will reset.",
"## Keep Photoshop updated, carefully",
"Updates often include performance fixes, but avoid updating in the middle of wedding season deadlines. Wait a week or two after a major release, check that your plugins still work, and keep the previous version installed if your Creative Cloud plan allows.",
"## Autosave and recovery",
"Large files take time to autosave, which can cause brief freezes. In File Handling preferences you can set the auto-recovery interval to 10 or 15 minutes instead of 5. Do not switch it off completely; losing an hour of work on a complex spread is worse than a short pause.",
],[("Why does Photoshop freeze when saving large album files?","Large files take time to compress and write. Turning off Maximize Compatibility and saving to an SSD helps."),("Does closing other programs help Photoshop speed?","Yes. Browsers and other heavy apps use RAM that Photoshop could otherwise use for large documents.")])
_ext('make-your-own-album-background-psd',[
"## Background styles for different events",
"- **Ceremony:** rich colours, traditional motifs and gold ornaments in the margins.",
"- **Reception:** darker tones, bokeh or subtle sparkle textures.",
"- **Pre-wedding:** light, airy pastels or soft nature textures.",
"- **Family collages:** simple, low-contrast backgrounds so many faces remain clear.",
"- **Kids and birthdays:** bright pastels, playful shapes and confetti patterns at low opacity.",
"Create one master background per style and you will cover most albums with a handful of files.",
"## Making backgrounds work with photo frames",
"Frames sit on top of backgrounds, so plan both together. A thin white or gold stroke around each frame separates photos from busy textures. Soft drop shadows with low opacity and a large blur radius add depth without looking dated. Keep frame styles consistent across the album.",
"## Common mistakes",
"### Too much contrast",
"High-contrast textures compete with photos. Reduce texture opacity until it is noticeable only when you look for it.",
"### Ornaments across the fold",
"Ornaments split by the fold look broken in the finished album. Place them on either side of the centre line.",
"### Low-resolution textures",
"Stretching a small texture across 10800 pixels makes it blurry. Use textures of at least 5000 pixels on the long side, or tile them as patterns.",
],[("What colours make good album backgrounds?","Softer versions of colours from the wedding, such as the saree or decor, usually support photos best."),("Can I sell album backgrounds I make myself?","Yes, if every element, including textures and ornaments, is your own work or licensed for resale.")])
_ext('wedding-album-captions-and-quotes',[
"## Writing a dedication page",
"A dedication page near the start or end of the album can carry a few lines from the couple: a thank-you to parents, a memory of how they met, or a hope for the future. Keep it to three to five sentences. Write it in the couple's voice and let them approve it word for word, since this is the most personal text in the album.",
"### Dedication examples to adapt",
"- To our parents, who gave us roots and wings. This album is for you.",
"- We met by chance and stayed by choice. Thank you to everyone who celebrated with us.",
"- For the families who became one on this day, with love and gratitude.",
"## Captions for chapter openers",
"Chapter titles help viewers navigate a long album. Short, consistent labels work best: The Engagement, Getting Ready, The Ceremony, Family, The Reception. Place them in the same position on each chapter's first spread, using the same font and size.",
"## Proofreading names and dates",
"Spelling mistakes in names are the most common text error in albums and the most upsetting for clients. Copy names from the wedding invitation rather than typing from memory, confirm initials and family names, and double-check dates in both English and regional calendars if used. Ask the client to approve all text on the final proof.",
],[("Where should a dedication page go in an album?","Usually after the title page or as the final page before the back cover."),("Should captions be in English or a regional language?","Either works. Many families like a mix: names and titles in English with one meaningful line in their language.")])
_ext('parents-album-design-guide',[
"## Covers for parents' albums",
"Parents' albums can share the main album's cover design at a smaller size, or use a simpler version with the couple's names and date. Some families like the family name or both families' names on the cover. Keep the photo and font choices consistent with the main album so the set looks like a matching collection.",
"## Planning photo counts",
"A typical parents' album has 10 to 20 sheets. With three to six photos per spread, that is roughly 50 to 100 photos. Start with ceremony and family portraits, then add a few couple portraits and reception highlights until you reach the agreed sheet count. It is easier to add photos than to remove them after parents have seen a draft.",
"## Avoiding common problems",
"- **Missing relatives:** cross-check the must-include list before sending the proof.",
"- **Too many couple shots:** parents often feel the album is not really for them.",
"- **Tiny faces:** smaller formats need fewer photos per page.",
"- **Mixed files at the lab:** keep separate folders and clear file names for each family.",
"## Delivering with the main album",
"Delivering parents' albums at the same time as the main album creates a memorable moment for the family and saves a second trip. Plan design time for all albums together in your schedule, and send them to the lab as one order where possible.",
],[("How many sheets should a parents' album have?","Most have 10 to 20 sheets, which holds roughly 50 to 100 photos."),("Should the parents' album have the same cover as the main album?","It often does at a smaller size, or a simpler version with names and date.")])
_ext('album-binding-and-lamination-guide',[
"## Cover materials",
"The cover is part of the binding decision. Common choices are a printed photo cover with lamination, leatherette, fabric or a padded cover with an acrylic or metal panel. Printed covers need a designed wrap file including spine and turn-in margins, while leatherette and fabric covers may only need a small panel image. Ask the lab which cover types they offer and request their exact template.",
"### Boxes and bags",
"Many studios supply albums in a matching box or carry bag. These protect the lamination during handling and make delivery feel premium. Some boxes accept a printed lid image; design it at the lab's specified size.",
"## Sheet thickness",
"Lay-flat albums come with different board thicknesses. Thicker boards feel premium and protect prints, but increase weight and spine width. For a 40-sheet 12x36 album, very thick boards can make the book heavy to handle, so medium thickness is a sensible default for large albums.",
"## Quality checks on delivery",
"When the album arrives from the lab, check every spread before handing it to the client: lamination bubbles, misaligned sheets at the fold, colour shifts between sheets, and trimming that cuts into text. Report problems to the lab immediately with photos of the affected pages.",
],[("Does lamination change album colours?","Yes. Glossy usually deepens colours, while matte softens contrast slightly. A test print on the chosen finish helps."),("How heavy is a 40-sheet 12x36 lay-flat album?","It can weigh several kilograms with thick boards, so medium thickness is often chosen for large albums.")])
_ext('album-design-client-revisions',[
"## A sample proof message",
"A clear proof message saves back-and-forth. For example: Here is the first draft of your album, 30 sheets plus cover. Please review each page and send all changes in one message by Saturday, listing the sheet number and the change. This draft includes two revision rounds as agreed. After your second round of changes we will send the final proof for approval.",
"## Timelines that work",
"Give clients a clear deadline for each round, usually five to seven days. Without a deadline, feedback can take months and albums sit unfinished. If a client misses the deadline, send a polite reminder and explain that the album will move back in your queue.",
"## Tools that help",
"- **Shared online galleries** for photo selection with favourites and comments.",
"- **Numbered PDF proofs** so feedback references sheet numbers.",
"- **A simple spreadsheet** to track each client's revision round and status.",
"- **Template naming conventions** so you can find and change any sheet quickly.",
"## Learning from revisions",
"After each album, note what clients changed most often. If many ask for more family photos, increase family collages in your first draft. If clients often change backgrounds, show two background options at the start. Over time, first drafts get closer to final and revisions shrink.",
],[("How long should clients get to review an album proof?","Five to seven days per round is common and keeps projects moving."),("What if a client keeps asking for changes after approval?","Refer to the agreed revision rounds and explain that further changes after approval may carry a fee and delay printing.")])
